a few people realize that before i made  even the first video for khan academy  the whole point of khan academy was to  be a exercise practice feedback  reporting engine for students for  parents and for teachers and the first  videos were really just a way to  complement that so even if we go back as  as far back as 2005 we had a lot of  exercises on khan academy when we got  our first real funding in 2010 that's  where we we invested most heavily  now that the common core has come out  and not only has it come out not only  have the standards been published but  now that the there's a sense of the  adoption there's a sense of how it is  going to be assessed  we've decided to do a serious push to  intentionally make sure that our  exercises match the common core now i  want to be very clear here our intention  isn't to just map to the common core  because a lot of states have happened to  adopt it our intention is not to map to  the to just superficially take some  content and and and do a a superficial  mapping to the common core  are the whole purpose of why we're doing  this is when we looked at the standards  when we looked at the items coming out  of the assessment consortia from smarter  balanced from park we realized that this  is a very very very good standard it's a  rigorous standard it's going to measure  real deep student understanding and  there's going to be a huge need for  students and teachers and parents to  have resources where they can  get practice get feedback get  explanations around common core subjects  and so what you see right over here is a  screen of our common core site this is a  place this is really a a dashboard where  teachers or parents or even students can  look at common core standards and then  think about which khan academy content  maps to that standard it's available at  khanacademy.org  common core and you could go by  different grade levels grade 7 grade 8  and you could see the different khan  academy exercises that cover that cover  that that standard and we're actually in  process on doing this right now  we have a team of teachers of professors  of graduate students who are working  closely with us working very closely  with many of the authors of the common  core itself to make sure that we we we  intentionally create content that really  hits at the conceptual spirit of the  common core and just as a sense of the  type of team we're looking at let me of  the type of folks who are working on  this with us let me show you our team  page  so if you go to the bottom of any khan  academy page and you go to our content  specialist you'll go to this page right  over here and this is just a this is a  sample of the folks who are working with  us many of them are experts in  developmental mathematics in early  learning some of them are graduate  students some of them are professors of  mathematics at the university level but  it's a really high quality of tea a high  quality team of folks who have been  helping us make sure that we have really  deep and really peer vetted content for  intentionally created for the common  core and once again it's not just so  because 40 something states have adopted  it we want we like the common core  because we think when properly  implemented and that's a very important  thing to say when properly implemented  it is really trying to measure deep  conceptual understanding and so i  encourage you to look at the bios of all  of these folks here and we're constantly  adding more and more people to  collaborate on this effort to see the  type of content that they're creating  it's anything from fairly simple  exercises like this rewrite the  expression 75 plus 20 in the following  form and the whole point of this is to  measure we'll just in order to test  whether to someone to really understand  the distributive property and we've  built tools and widgets so that we can  do free expression entry around it so  for example 75 plus 20 we would want to  factor out let's see we could factor out  a 5 and that's 5 times  5 times we could write that as 25  or 15 i should say 15  plus 4.  and i should write this down as a  multiplication symbol just like this and  there you have it we've written this in  the form that they wanted and we can  check our answer so this is just a very  simple widget to understand does someone  understand factoring and kind of undoing  the distributive property but then  there's more complicated problems and  this is really the spirit of the common  core a lot of people a lot of times in  an algebra class you'll have people  talking about functions evaluating  functions but the spirit of the common  core is to really understand what a  function is saying and really  constructing functions so this is an  example of a word problem  an application problem where the student  has to interpret the function to make  some fairly fairly nuanced conclusions  about what's going to happen given the  data that's going on  we also have things like this that are  that are widgets that actually kind of  are are for like the word virtual  manipulatives to help build these these  these content items so for example here  move each expression to the right  category to indicate if each one is less  than equal to or greater than 6 7. so  the student will say okay let's see 5 7  plus 4 7 that's 9 7.  that's greater than  6 7's let's see 2 plus let's see this is  going to be 5 7 that's less than this  right over here is going to be let's see  this is going to be exactly equal to 6 7  and you get the general idea over here  and then we have things like this and  these are all written by experts in  their field these are written by people  who are looking very closely at the  standards looking they're working with  specialists they're working with some of  the folks who've even written the  standards and being trained by the folks  who've been written the standards to  come up with these questions to once  again really go at the conceptual  understanding that the standard is  trying to uh call out so over here in  the number 47 339 how many times less is  the value of the second three  then the value of the first three so the  second three represents 30 it's in the  tens place it's three tens this three is  in the hundreds place it represents  three hundreds so thirty is ten times  less than three hundred so you would you  type in ten so this is just an example  where actually just just coming this  fall we have a hundred we have on the  order of a hundred exercises that have  been intentionally created for the  common core and it's not just the  exercises but on top of that we have a  whole series of videos that have been  intentionally created for the common  core as well so for example this one is  interpreting features of functions we  have a whole series of videos that  explain the the underlying idea behind  what's going on so as we go over the  next few months and and definitely over  the next year especially because fall of  2014 is when you're going to have major  adoption of the common core in most of  the country  the goal is to have all of the major  standards covered from k through high  school and then even beyond for us to  really be a super set of the common core  and that's the goal by next fall even  this fall we want to cover most of the  major standards starting this fall going  through middle school and we're going to  be aggressively adding content above and  beyond that and for regions whether it's  states or or countries that are not  using the common core i want to  emphasize that we're not doing this just  to cater to the common core states we  want to do this because we want to have  a rigor we want to have rigorous math  content and exercises on the site that  would hopefully allow students to  succeed in any environment that they  that they're in so we are really  intending to be a super set not a not a  exact covering of the common core
 

